WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.692 --> 00:00:01.650
DEAN HILDEBRAND: Hello.

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:05.100
Today we're going to give a
peek behind the VM's you launch

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.170
on Google Cloud into our
storage infrastructure.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.910
My name is Dean
Hildebrand, and I'm

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.550
a technical director for
storage in the Google Cloud

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.620
office of the CTO.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.300
DENIS SERENYI: And
I'm Denis Serenyi.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.325
I'm the tech lead for
Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:21.895 --> 00:00:23.020
DEAN HILDEBRAND: All right.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.760
So in this session,
we're going to answer

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.810
a very simple question--
how does Google Cloud scale

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.520
your storage infrastructure?

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.710
And it actually has a
relatively simple answer.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.350
So the first key
point, and I really

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.020
do think this is a
very important point,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.450
is that there is a single
infrastructure that

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.810
underpins both Google
services and Google Cloud.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.870
And so Google Cloud
customers, and YouTube,

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:50.170
and [? Android ?] Play
Store services scale

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.120
for the exact same
reason, and that's

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.550
because they're using the exact
same underlying infrastructure.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.710
And next, we're going to dive
into the details of Colossus,

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.190
the file system that is at
the core of this scalability

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.300
and talk about how Colossus
abstracts out the complexities

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.760
and messiness of the
physical infrastructure

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.760
to provide a highly-performant,
cost-efficient storage

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.790
infrastructure for Google
Cloud's storage services.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.880
So let's take a step back.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.940
What are Cloud regions?

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.470
With 23 regions and 70 zones
working together so seamlessly,

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.590
it's sometimes easy to forget
they're actually mapped down

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.520
to a set of groups of
physical data centers,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.800
and what's in
these data centers?

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:37.610
Pipes.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.870
Lots of pipes?

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.950
But actually, OK, there's
a little more as well.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.010
There's also lots of
servers and storage,

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.890
because how else are you
going to compute anything?

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.350
So it's pretty straightforward,
though, that when

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:53.750
you launch a VM, let's
say, a 32 VCPU skylight

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.020
ends up running on one
of our skylight machines.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.790
And the operation of this
is pretty fascinating,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.540
but one of the questions
I get a lot from customers

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.490
is, how does our storage work?

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.850
How does Google Cloud take
our servers, disks, networks,

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.210
and cobble them all together
to provide such a wide range

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:12.940
of storage services?

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.920
And a typical follow-up
question is, does Google

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.350
use the same infrastructure
as Google Cloud?

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:22.110
And of course, the answer is
yes, but how does this work?

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.470
So let's cover that as well.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.670
So in this talk, we wanted
to give a peek behind the VM

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:32.090
that you're launching to give
some insight into how we build

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.740
our storage systems
here at Google

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.550
so you can have the confidence
that your applications

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.775
on Google Cloud can scale
just like Google services.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.270
So, to begin, just like any
well-designed software system,

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.240
all of Google is layered
with a common set

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.650
of scalable services.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:55.650
There are three
core building blocks

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.900
used by each of our
storage services

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.302
and they are Colossus,
Spanner, and Borg.

00:03:00.302 --> 00:03:02.010
And then, of course,
there's many others,

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.790
but in the interest of time,
let's focus on these big three.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.120
So first we have Borg,
which launches everything

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.730
from compute services
to storage services

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.380
and everything in between.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.260
And as probably many
of you already know,

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.350
much of the learnings
inside of Borg

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.580
was all translated into
Kubernetes when it launched.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.980
Second, we have
Spanner, which is

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.550
our globally-consistent
scalable database that

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:34.660
is used by Google services as
well as our Cloud customers

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.000
as Cloud Spanner.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.340
And finally, we have
Colossus, the beginnings

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.770
of which started
as the Google file

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.200
system which inspired
many open source file

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.960
systems such as HDFS.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.870
But since that time,
Google has scaled a lot,

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.220
and there is a lot
more data to store.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.390
And so the need for this
higher level of scalability

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.630
really drove the
creation of Colossus.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:00.760
And since this is a
talk about storage,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.730
Dennis is going to go deep
into the details of Colossus

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:04.490
in a few moments.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.320
And so to get back to
this question of how

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.420
does Google Cloud
operate our storage,

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.899
it uses these three
core building blocks

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:19.930
to provide the underpinnings
for all of the storage services

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:23.890
that you see from
Firestore, to Cloud SQL,

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:28.120
to Filestore, and
Google Cloud Storage.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.820
Now, let's also get
back to the question

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.420
of whether Google uses
the same infrastructure,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.033
and how does it use it?

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:37.700
And the answer, of
course, is that, yes,

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.180
it does, as I've stated already.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.430
And it does it in a pretty
simple way, in the same way

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:43.388
that the Cloud does it.

00:04:43.388 --> 00:04:45.820
It takes each of the
services that you

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.700
see, from YouTube to Photos--

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.960
takes these same scalable
building blocks and then layers

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.630
on top of all of
their needs to provide

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:59.740
the level of availability,
performance, durability

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:02.510
that you're looking for
in your storage systems.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:06.520
So this allows both Google
services and Google Cloud

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.770
customers to scale
in the exact same way

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.590
because they're using the exact
same set of building blocks.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:15.580
And so you can have the
confidence that if you go off

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.370
and watch Cobra Kai and there's
also a million other people

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.010
that want to watch Cobra
Kai at the same time,

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:23.920
then these three services
are behind the scenes scaling

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.450
to meet the needs.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.640
And traditionally,
what's interesting

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.670
is that much of the use
cases into Borg, Spanner,

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:34.180
and Colossus, and all of
the innovation in them

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.410
was driven by the
Google services.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:41.800
But as our Cloud customers have
really scaled across the globe,

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.810
they are now providing a lot
of the innovation as well.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.400
So let's look at a typical flow.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.230
Let's say user
launches a GKE cluster

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:55.490
on to a series of servers.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:59.320
So the VMs in that cluster
get launched on to a series

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:00.940
of the servers that
they requested--

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:03.230
and that's using
Borg, by the way--

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:06.280
and so then these VMs running
on those services in the GKE

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.310
cluster access their
favorite storage services.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:13.570
Let's say they use Persistent
Disk for their boot volume,

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.330
and then they used Filestore
and Google Cloud Storage

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.460
for their unstructured data.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.890
Once they start
accessing those services,

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:23.860
the exact same big
three building blocks--

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.870
Colossus, Spanner, and Borg--

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:28.970
are underneath the covers
providing all of the services.

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:32.320
So Borg is provisioning the
resources for that storage

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.290
system, Spanner is
storing the metadata,

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:38.140
to say, for example,
who can access the data

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.600
and where is the data
actually located,

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.210
and then Colossus, of
course, is providing

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:44.970
all storage for the data.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.680
So now that we understand
these three basic services,

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:54.430
for the rest of this talk, let's
deep-dive into Cloud Storage

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:56.650
and how it makes
use of Colossus.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.080
So from the user
point of view, Google

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.060
has spent a lot
of time and effort

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:05.260
to try to make Cloud Storage
as easy to use as possible.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:08.860
We want it to be as easy as
filling up and emptying out

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.510
an actual bucket.

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:15.200
But behind the scenes,
from Google's perspective,

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:18.770
Cloud Storage is actually a
massive distributed system

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:20.510
that has the potential
to take advantage

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:23.420
of all of our regions,
networks, points of presence,

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:26.340
and all the servers and disks
that I talked about earlier.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.030
And it has to make use
of all those aspects

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:32.360
to satisfy three
primary use cases--

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.640
content storage,
compute analytics,

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.113
and machine learning, as
well as backup and archive.

00:07:38.113 --> 00:07:39.530
And from the Cloud
perspective, we

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:41.840
have lots of customers
in each of these areas

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:43.830
that are scaling,
using the services.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:46.610
So, for example, box is
delivering a lot of content

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:49.010
to its users, the
Broad Institute

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:54.440
is constantly analyzing lots
of different genomes every day,

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:57.560
and The New York Times, you
can find a really interesting

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:01.550
article about how it's using
Google Cloud Storage to archive

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:04.670
its entire photo history.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:07.070
But Google Cloud
Storage can scale

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:08.870
to meet these needs
because it's already

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:16.190
been meeting the needs of our
services such as YouTube, ads,

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:21.390
and photos, which
archives a lot of photos.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:25.610
So in this talk, we've
learned about how Colossus

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:27.770
is underpinning
Google Cloud Storage,

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:31.825
and it provides support for
three very unique use cases.

00:08:31.825 --> 00:08:33.200
And now I'm going
to turn it over

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:36.380
to Denis so he can
explain, how does Colossus

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.960
achieve all of this?

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.400
DENIS SERENYI: Thanks, Dean.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.830
So we talk a lot
about GCS and how

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.440
it can support a very
wide range of use cases

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.250
from archival storage to
high throughput analytics,

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:51.680
but we don't talk a lot
about the system that lies

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.860
under GCS, Colossus,
which enables

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:58.710
GCS's unique capabilities.

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:00.590
So I'm going to dive
into the details

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:05.130
of how GCS uses Colossus
and how Colossus works.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.710
We'll start from the top
and work our way down.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.460
So I'm starting with the
client library which is how

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.070
GCS interacts with Colossus.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:16.130
Actually, the client is
probably the most complex part

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:17.490
of the system.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.270
There's a lot of functionality
that goes directly in there,

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:23.210
for instance, software
RAID, and applications

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.010
can choose their
own encodings based

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.890
on application requirements.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:31.190
Applications built on top
of Colossus, such as GCS,

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:35.375
use a variety of encodings to
fine tune the performance cost

00:09:35.375 --> 00:09:40.540
trade-offs that they need to
make for different workloads.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:44.140
The foundation of Colossus is
its scalable metadata service

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:45.970
called the curator.

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:49.210
It scales because clients
talk to it only for control

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:52.390
operations-- for instance,
creating a file--

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:56.500
and because it can
scale out horizontally.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.350
It can also scale
massively because it's

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:03.210
built on top of a NoSQL
database like Bigtable.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.220
In fact, the original
motive Google

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.560
had for building
Colossus was that search

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:10.740
was running into scaling
limits, as Dean mentioned,

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:14.370
of its predecessor,
the Google file system.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:16.590
Putting file system
metadata into Bigtable

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:18.870
was the key trick
that allowed Colossus

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:25.450
to scale up by over 100x over
the largest DFS clusters.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:27.970
Another way the system
scales is that Colossus

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:31.690
minimizes the number of hops
for data on the network.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:34.790
Data flows directly
between D servers,

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:37.660
which our really
not very clever name

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:43.440
for our simple network, detach
disks and the Colossus client.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.730
Finally, Colossus includes
background storage managers

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:49.340
called custodians
which handle things

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:53.090
like disk space balancing
and RAID reconstruction.

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:55.970
They play a key role in
maintaining the durability

00:10:55.970 --> 00:11:00.290
and availability of data as well
as ensuring the storage system

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:03.450
is working efficiently.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.490
So that's Colossus
in a nutshell.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:07.490
Here's a picture of
what a typical Google

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:09.410
cluster looks like.

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:13.190
The key ingredient is having
the shared storage pool managed

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:15.680
by the Colossus control plane.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.290
This large, disaggregated
storage layer

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:21.620
is able to support a wide
variety of applications

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:24.730
on the same underlying
physical resources

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.680
and each application
has the rough illusion

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:30.560
of having an
isolated file system.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:33.920
In aggregate, we're talking
about really big scale here.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.490
A single cluster is scalable
to the exabytes of storage

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:40.640
with tens of
thousands of machines.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.970
And what I've drawn here
represents a typical mix

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:46.220
of internal Google services.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:51.050
You've got some YouTube serving
and maybe some Ads MapReduces.

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:56.510
Together with GCP VM instances,
using GCS, altogether

00:11:56.510 --> 00:12:00.930
sharing the same underlying
Colossus file system.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:03.770
So it's really the case that
the same storage system that's

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:05.690
driving these
global scale Google

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:10.600
applications is also driving
our customers' Cloud workloads.

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:14.270
The scale desegregation
of resources and variety

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:17.960
of workloads is the key to
driving efficiency and lowering

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:19.130
costs.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:21.890
For instance, we can
provision to the peak demand

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:24.980
of low latency workloads, like
maybe this YouTube serving

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:28.070
workload here, and then
run batch workloads

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:31.340
like MapReduces
and other analytics

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:34.610
more cheaply by having them
fill in the gaps of otherwise

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:35.270
idle time.

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:39.830
Another great thing
about Colossus

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:42.830
is it abstracts away
a ton of complexity

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:45.800
that storage intensive
applications would otherwise

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:47.360
have to deal with.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.910
For one thing, frankly,
our data centers

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:53.870
have a tremendous variety of
underlying storage hardware.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:55.970
When we're adding
capacity to the system,

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:58.670
we're always buying the
disk of the day, which

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:01.040
is whatever disk
currently has the lowest

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:05.240
total cost of ownership,
and the same goes for flash.

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:07.440
And these devices
last a long time,

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.730
which means that each cluster
will have many disk sizes

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:13.250
and types.

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:15.620
Add to that the fact
that applications

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:18.080
have different requirements
around durability,

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:20.210
availability, and latency.

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:23.450
Colossus addresses that
by providing service tiers

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:26.780
and allowing applications to
specify erasure codes tuned

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:29.570
to their needs.

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:32.270
And add to that,
at Google scale,

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:35.480
some hardware is failing
virtually all the time.

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:38.510
That's just a testament to the
scale that we're operating at.

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:40.880
It's not like the
hardware is unreliable,

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:42.380
it's just that there
is a lot of it.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:46.310
So dealing with some
failures is the common case.

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:49.460
Colossus steers IO
around these failures

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:51.980
and does fast
background recovery

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:54.950
to provide highly durable
storage within the cluster

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:58.280
despite all of this chaos.

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:01.760
The end result is that for
GCS as well as Google's

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:04.820
own services, these
hardware resources

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:09.480
and their associated complexity
are totally abstracted away.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:14.100
Systems provision bytes and IO
as abstract, undifferentiated

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:15.300
units.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:18.917
That makes using storage very
simple for all the applications

00:14:18.917 --> 00:14:19.500
we're running.

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:25.640
So now, let's deep-dive
into how Colossus

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:30.110
helps ensure the storage pool
is being managed efficiently.

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:33.290
I mentioned that applications
built on top of Colossus can

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:37.280
provision bytes in IO
as abstract resources,

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:40.460
but as you might imagine,
it involves a lot of magic

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:44.360
to ensure that all the
IO and all the bytes

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.270
are available when
applications need them,

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:50.090
but also not over-provision
either of these things,

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:54.140
because that would
mean a lot of waste.

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:56.690
Under the hood, we take
advantage of the fact

00:14:56.690 --> 00:15:00.530
that data has a wide variety
of temperatures as we're

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:02.600
trying to illustrate here.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:06.530
We mix in flash, not always
to lower latency, but simply

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:09.020
because flash is the
most efficient way

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:11.540
to serve really hot data.

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:14.000
If we do our job right,
we buy just enough

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:19.640
flash to push the IO density per
gigabyte for the remaining data

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:22.850
into the range that
can be served by disks,

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:24.830
and then we buy
just enough disks

00:15:24.830 --> 00:15:26.960
so that we can fill them all up.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:29.540
If we do this well and
have the right mix,

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:33.700
we end up with really
efficient storage.

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:35.650
Now let's talk
about how Colossus

00:15:35.650 --> 00:15:39.880
maximizes storage efficiency
for disk-based storage.

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:43.220
Within our population of
disks, as I mentioned earlier,

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:46.450
we're going to have a wide
variety of disk sizes.

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:49.960
To maximize efficiency we'll
want to do two things--

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:51.820
we'll want to keep
the disks full,

00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:55.210
because empty bytes are
just excess inventory,

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:57.190
and we want to keep
the disks busy,

00:15:57.190 --> 00:16:00.430
so we're not wasting
that disk spindle.

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:04.720
Because each cluster has
roughly the same IO bandwidth,

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:07.060
what this means is that--

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:09.910
sorry, because each disk
has roughly the same IO

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:11.920
bandwidth, what this
means is that we'll

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:14.860
have to keep hotter
data evenly spread

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:17.720
across all the drives
in the cluster,

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:20.230
and then we just fill up
all the remaining space

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:23.690
on each drive with cold bytes.

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:25.820
To make this work,
the key problem

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:30.110
that Colossus has solved is
placing data intelligently.

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:33.050
Turns out, newly written
data is typically hotter,

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:37.370
so Colossus spreads new writes
evenly across all the disks,

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:41.720
leading to a pretty even
balance of hot data.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:44.420
And then Colossus's his
background rebalancer

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:48.590
moves older, cold
data to bigger drives.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:50.690
This works great for
analytics workloads,

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:55.700
because analytics data
typically cools off as it ages.

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:58.610
Now, a lot of this stuff
was developed and refined

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:02.000
over the years so that Google
can run its own services--

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.000
search ads, YouTube, et
cetera, all those services

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:06.740
that Dean was talking about--

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:11.520
at massive scale, as simply
and efficiently as possible.

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:14.569
But for Cloud, this means
that Cloud users can also

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:17.000
leverage this functionality,
and you end up

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:19.115
with simple and
inexpensive storage.

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:25.910
OK, so this is a picture of
one of our internal dashboards

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:28.310
used to monitor
and debug storage

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:30.500
performance in a cluster.

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:33.590
There's a lot of boxes
in this visualization.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:36.830
Each of those small
boxes is a D server,

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:39.470
and their sized by disk
capacity and colored

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:41.840
by spindle utilization.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:44.760
When it looks like,
this we're really happy.

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:48.120
Basically, the whole
cluster is really busy--

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:50.790
that's why it's looking
all red like that--

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:54.590
so we're getting all the value
out of our disk spindles.

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:57.090
Sure, there's some darker
colors sprinkled in there,

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:59.240
but it turns out those
are D servers that

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:03.830
are down for maintenance
or being decommissioned.

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:05.780
So that's Colossus
in a nutshell.

00:18:05.780 --> 00:18:10.340
Now, let's pop the stack, and
talk about GCS itself, which

00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:13.280
is really just a layer on top.

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:17.300
It turns out, GCS is employing
a lot of the same tricks

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:22.820
that Colossus uses, just at the
region and multi-region level.

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:25.100
To illustrate how
they're similar, here's

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:28.310
a simple representation
of a GCS deployment in one

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:30.470
of our GCP regions.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:33.350
In this example, we've
got three clusters,

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:38.580
each with a GCS instance
on top of Colossus storage.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:42.810
First off, GCS employs some
of the same hot data placement

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:46.470
techniques used by Colossus--
placing data close to where

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:50.010
it's likely to be read
and also caching data

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:53.025
to minimize network
distance and lower latency.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:59.380
And just like Colossus is
rebalancing colder data

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:03.610
within the cluster, GCS
rebalances colder data

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:06.100
between clusters in
the region to make

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:10.720
sure we're able to fully
utilize all available space

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:14.590
as well as ensuring that data
is always durable and available

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:17.230
in the face of
disruptions or failures

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:21.250
in the underlying
infrastructure.

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:24.550
GCS kind of thinks of each
cluster in Google's fleet

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:28.690
as an abstract disk, and
by managing these clusters

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:31.270
intelligently,
it's able to handle

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:34.830
all the diverse workloads
that users bring to GCS,

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:39.010
like content serving in
archival and analytics,

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:41.170
and do it efficiently.

00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:46.740
The way we think of GCS is that
it's Colossus in the Cloud.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:50.080
So to wrap up, we know
about GCP's scalability.

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:52.340
What we uncovered
today is that GCP

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:55.550
can scale because it's built
on top of these core Google

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:58.160
services that are
battle tested and have

00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:02.910
demonstrated the ability to
scale to pretty massive levels.

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:07.190
In fact, GCS is just a
layer on top of Colossus,

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:10.010
which is the cluster level
file system that doesn't just

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:14.960
handle the storage needs of GCE
VMs, GCS content serving, et

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:19.130
cetera, but also handles all of
Google's internal storage needs

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:24.850
from Search, to Maps,
YouTube, photos, et cetera.

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:27.820
And lastly, we learn that
there is a ton of complexity

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:31.450
under the covers of GCP,
but systems like Colossus

00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:33.580
have abstracted
all of that away,

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:38.090
allowing GCP customers to
just focus on their business.

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:40.750
So to wrap up, I want to
give a big thanks to Dean

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:43.510
for being my partner in
crime for this deep-dive

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:47.355
into what lies under the
hood of storage at GCP.

00:20:47.355 --> 00:20:50.680
DEAN: Hey, Denis, glad to be
that partner, and thanks a ton

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:53.830
for sharing your extremely
deep knowledge in Colossus

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:56.440
and Google Cloud
Storage, and I really

00:20:56.440 --> 00:20:59.320
hope this gives our
customers confidence that,

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:02.080
by using this underlying
scalable infrastructure,

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:04.570
that they can scale their
applications as big as they're

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:05.976
looking for.

00:21:05.976 --> 00:21:07.380
DENIS: Thanks a lot.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:09.470
I hope you found this
as interesting as we do

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:11.160
and that you learned a lot.

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:13.120
Have a great day.

